    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, quick question for you. We just bought a new CNC milling machine for the shop. It cost us $85,000, and we're estimating it'll have a useful life of 10 years, with a salvage value of $5,000 at the end of that. Can you walk me through how the straight-line depreciation calculation works for this so I can get it into the year-end reports correctly?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To calculate straight-line depreciation, you first find the depreciable cost by subtracting the salvage value from the initial cost ($\\$85,000 - \\$5,000 = \\$80,000$). Then, you divide this depreciable cost by the useful life in years ($\\$80,000 / 10 \\text{ years} = \\$8,000$ per year). Therefore, your annual straight-line depreciation expense for this CNC machine is $\\$8,000$.",
      "source": "target",
